{"id":"ec6b1cca-2c2d-450d-ab6e-ff6eda0be9de","object":"chat.completion","created":1793258743,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"\n\n```python\nstats_res = read_file(\"deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py\")\nprint(stats_res['file_hash'])\nprint(len(stats_res['content']))\nhead = read_range(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", 1, 32)\nprint(head['content'])\n```","reasoning_content":"We have the content of environ.py and logger.py. Need full read of stats_logger.py and baseline pytest. Also need to see the full content? The read_file content is retained? The tool result says versioned evidence retained separately and only excerpts shown. But the earlier output actually printed? It says \"[versioned evidence retained separately]\" meaning the content wasn't shown in conversation? Hmm. The initial action printed env_res['content'] and logger_res['content'], but output shows only hashes and \"[versioned evidence retained separately]\". So full text not visible. However evidence headers gave key excerpts: environ lines 1-130 (partial), factory methods, rearm functions; logger full class and init function. We have enough mechanisms for logger and environ. Need stats_logger full read. Also baseline pytest.\n\nThe plan: first read three required files. environ and logger read via read_file (done). stats_logger not yet full read via read_file; evidence excerpts only. Need read_file stats_logger. Then baseline pytest. Then edit.\n\nWe should use read_file for stats_logger. Also maybe need full environ/logger contents to craft exact edits. We have enough for environ: factory methods and class envs. For logger: formatter and _setup_logger. But to edit precisely need exact text. We have excerpts with line numbers and exact code. Good.\n\nNeed read stats_logger full file (read_file). And possibly read environ lines around 130-230 to see comments/structure, but rearm provided. To insert new config we need exact location in envs class; we have last lines about MTP maybe partial. Could add after ACCELERATOR or after Runtime. Simpler add near Platform? Need exact unique anchor. We can use anchor `    # -- Platform --\\n    DEEPINFER_ACCELERATOR = EnvVar.str(default=\"nvidia\")` and add before Platform maybe. But env var is logging. Add:\n\n    # -- Logging --\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(int_or_none_validated)\n\nNeed a factory method in descriptor class. Requirements: new property same name, descriptor mechanism; unset None; set parsed int >=32, empty/unparseable/<32 raise ValueError containing variable name. Note descriptor wraps exceptions with name, so error text will include name anyway. But validation should raise ValueError. Also cache. Need factory method `int_or_none_min(...)`? We can add a static factory method e.g.:\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{v!r} must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n\nEmpty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError, wrapped with name. Unparseable ValueError. Less than 32 ValueError. Fine. Bool not relevant. Ensure parsing int, not float.\n\nDescriptor class is generic `class EnvVar[T]`. Python 3.12? likely. Add method.\n\nBut note `_descriptors` is typed `dict[str, EnvVar[Any]]` but EnvVar defined after; with future annotations okay.\n\nFor logger: extend NewLineFormatter __init__ with keyword-only `max_message_chars: int | None = None`. Must validate: if not None, must be int >=32 and not bool, else ValueError. Store. In format, first do super().format(record) which sets `record.message` and appends exception/stack info. We need compute length of message after args substitution, before prefix; only message body. We must not mutate shared record's msg/args etc. `super().format()` sets record.message but that's fine? Requirement says cannot rewrite msg, args, exc_info, stack_info. `record.message` is derived and can be modified? Better not use record.message for truncation after super().format because it may include exception text? Actually logging.Formatter.format: record.message = record.getMessage(); s = self.formatMessage(record); if record.exc_info: append exception text; if stack_info append. So `record.message` is only message body (args substituted), no exception/stack. Good. We can truncate a local copy of record.message and apply prefix replacement? But msg = super().format(record) already includes full message, exception, stack. Need output with truncated body and original prefix, exception, stack untouched. Approach: compute message = record.getMessage() before super? Actually we can call super().format then reconstruct? `msg` contains prefix + full message + exception text. To truncate only body, we can use record.message to find the body within msg. Since record.message is a substring of msg? Format string `_FORMAT` includes `%(message)s`, so yes record.message occurs. Multi-line handling uses `msg.find(record.message)`.\n\nSimplest: truncate record.message? But record.message is shared record attribute on the same LogRecord; if we set record.message to truncated, then later full handler receives same record and calls getMessage again, recomputes record.message from original msg/args — so no lasting damage. But requirement says \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info\" — record.message not in that list. But perhaps better not modify record.message at all. Yet we can use local.\n\nImplement:\n\n    def format(self, record):\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        message = record.message\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n            if message != record.message:\n                # replace only the first/appropriate occurrence of full body within msg?\n                idx = msg.find(record.message)\n                if idx >= 0:\n                    msg = msg[:idx] + message + msg[idx+len(record.message):]\n        # multi-line prefix\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]\n            ...\nBut now msg's message may be truncated; record.message full. `msg.find(record.message)` fails. Existing code uses record.message to compute prefix. Could use `message` local for prefix:\n\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]  # after replacement not works\nUse message variable:\n\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and message:\n            # prefix is before the first occurrence of message\n            idx = msg.find(message)\n            if idx > 0:\n                prefix = msg[:idx]\n                msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\nBut msg.replace replaces all newlines including those in message and exception, that's original behavior. Prefix computed from message start. If message empty? guard.\n\nAlternative order: do truncation on `record.message` copy then construct message via formatMessage? Hmm.\n\nBetter implement truncation before super().format by temporarily setting record.message? Actually super().format does that. Could call `super().format(record)` then use `record.message`.\n\nNeed exception handling: `msg` includes exception text after message. `msg.find(record.message)` find body; replace body with truncated; exception unchanged. But if record.message appears in prefix accidentally? Prefix is timestamp/level, unlikely. Good.\n\nThen multi-line prefix: original prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)] before replacement, but after replacement we lost full record.message. We can compute prefix before replacement. We can compute prefix from msg and record.message before truncating. Let's write:\n\n    def format(self, record):\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        body = record.message\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            truncated = _truncate_message(body, self.max_message_chars)\n            if truncated != body:\n                idx = msg.find(body)\n                if idx != -1:\n                    msg = msg[:idx] + truncated + msg[idx + len(body):]\n            body = truncated  # for prefix calculation? The message body now truncated.\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and body:\n            idx = msg.find(body)\n            if idx != -1:\n                prefix = msg[:idx]\n                msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n        ...\nBut if message contains newline and prefix computed; body may be truncated but still occurs. Fine. If body empty, no prefix. Use existing semantics.\n\nHowever exception text might contain the body string? find first occurrence; prefix before body. Good.\n\nTruncation helper:\n\n_OMITTED = \"...<omitted>...\"\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= max_chars:\n        return message\n    marker = \"...<omitted>...\"\n    remaining = max_chars - len(marker)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + marker + (message[-tail:] if tail > 0 else \"\")\n\nCheck example budget 32, marker 15, remaining 17, head 9, tail 8. Yes. marker length? \"...<omitted>...\" = 3+9? Let's count: \"...\"=3, \"<omitted>\" = 9 ( '<'1 + omitted7 + '>'1 =9), \"...\"=3 total 15. Good.\n\nNeed ensure max_message_chars >= len(marker)? Since >=32, fine.\n\n`max_message_chars` keyword-only: def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars: int | None = None). Validate bool: `isinstance(max_message_chars, bool)` or not isinstance(int). `if max_message_chars is not None and (isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32): raise ValueError(...)`. Error message maybe contains variable name? Requirement for logger says raises ValueError, not necessarily name, but could use DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS.\n\nDefault logger init: `_setup_logger` read config. Need import envs in logger. Where is import? We haven't seen top lines of logger. Need read logger lines 1-32 to see imports. We'll do read_range. Also `_setup_logger` exact anchor. From evidence, we have it.\n\nImportant: config access may raise ValueError during init_logger if env invalid. That's expected.\n\n`_setup_logger` repeated initialization must not add handler twice (already). It creates new formatter each time, applying current cached config. \"重新执行默认日志初始化时，将当时的配置值应用到新的格式化器；已创建的旧格式化器保留原预算。\" So in `_setup_logger`, always create `NewLineFormatter(..., max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS)`. Access config property. But if env unset, None. Good.\n\nBut what about module import? `logger.py` may be imported by environ? no. Add `from deepinfer.environ import envs` top. Need see imports.\n\nStats logger: `_log_worker` line 116-126: `logger.info(m)` -> `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`. That's it. Need full read stats_logger first per required_full_reads. Let's read it and logger top. Also run baseline pytest may be done after read. The required order: read all three files with read_file first, then read/reuse complete mechanisms before first edit. So next: read_file stats_logger and read_range logger 1-32, maybe environ 130-230 for rearm exact? Already have. Let's call read_file stats_logger, read_range logger 1-32. Then baseline pytest. Need be careful not to exceed output.\n\nWe also need initial full file_read records for all three. environ and logger done via read_file. stats_logger via read_file now. The full content is retained in result but not printed; okay. We don't need print full content. We should print hash and maybe some parts. Let's read."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":7773,"completion_tokens":2690,"total_tokens":10463,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":3968},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2627},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":3968,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":3805},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}